# Python
#
# The directory patterns below are anchored with a leading slash so they match Python
# packaging output at the project root and nothing else. Unanchored, `build/` also matched
# `scripts/build/`, which quietly excluded build-dist.sh and docker-push.sh: both were
# documented in scripts/README.md and neither existed in a fresh clone. This is the same
# failure the `.cargo/*` note below describes — a directory excluded here is not descended
# into, so nothing underneath it can be negated back in.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
/build/
/develop-eggs/
/dist/
/downloads/
/eggs/
/.eggs/
/lib/
/lib64/
/parts/
/sdist/
/var/
/wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg
MANIFEST
.env
venv/
ENV/
env/
.venv
pip-log.txt
pip-delete-this-directory.txt
.tox/
.coverage
.coverage.*
.cache
nosetests.xml
coverage.xml
*.cover
.hypothesis/
.pytest_cache/

# Runtime data directories, and any copy of one made by hand.
#
# Naming the two subdirectories the server creates (/data/cameodb/, /data/examples/) left a
# renamed or backed-up copy tracked: moving data/cameodb aside as data/cameodb.bak-<ts> to get
# a clean test run put a database — redb files, tantivy segments, node_identity.json — into
# `git status`. So match the directory itself and everything under it, whatever it is called.
#
# Anchored to the repository root on purpose, so it never catches a `data` directory nested
# somewhere in the tree. `examples/data/` is checked in and must stay that way.
/data*/

# Test artifacts and temporary files
*.tmp
*.temp

# Scripts are committed but their logs/outputs are ignored
!/scripts/
scripts/**/*.log
scripts/**/*.tmp

# Environment and configuration
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# Runtime and cache directories
.cache/
.rustup/

# .cargo/config.toml is build configuration, not a cache: it carries the musl link and
# hardening flags and jemalloc's page size. Ignoring the whole directory meant a fresh clone
# silently built release binaries with none of them. Credentials stay ignored.
#
# .cargo/audit.toml is policy for the same reason: it records which advisories are accepted
# and why. `scripts/validate/deps.sh` runs `cargo audit`, which reads it, so a clone without
# it fails on exceptions this repo has already reviewed.
#
# `.cargo/*` rather than `.cargo/` on purpose — git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so a negation cannot re-include a file underneath one.
.cargo/*
!.cargo/config.toml
!.cargo/audit.toml

# Backup files
*.orig
*.rej

# Profiling and debugging
*.prof
perf.data*
flamegraph.svg

# Documentation build artifacts
# Generated documentation should not be committed:
# - target/doc/ (rustdoc output) - already covered by /target/
# - book/ (mdbook output)
# - docs/book/ (if using mdbook in docs/)
# - docs/web/ (Next.js website build artifacts and node_modules)
# 
# Source documentation SHOULD be committed:
# - docs/*.md (our architecture and API docs)
# - README.md files
# - Inline /// documentation in source code
book/
docs/book/
docs/web/
docs/webpage

# Local development overrides
local.toml
dev.toml
cameodb.toml
cameodb.yaml
